library org.openxtalk.library.coin

-- coinxt.lcb - the LiveCode Builder binding for CoinXT (public cx* surface).
--
-- This is the FFI seam and nothing else: one `private foreign handler` per
-- cnx_* symbol in native/coinxt.c, wrapped in a `public cx*` handler that
-- allocates the output buffer, marshals the bytes, checks the native status,
-- and throws a legible "CoinXT: ..." error. Encodings (hex, Base58Check,
-- Bech32, RLP, addresses) deliberately do NOT live here: they are checksummed
-- byte-shuffling with no secret-dependent branch, so they belong in the
-- livecodescript layer where they are diffable and KAT-covered (CLAUDE.md,
-- "The C-vs-script split").
--
-- Phase 1 scope: the hash surface (Keccak-256, SHA3-256, SHA-256, SHA-512,
-- RIPEMD-160, HMAC-SHA256/512, PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA512), their length accessors,
-- and the ABI guard.
-- Phase 2 scope (ABI 3): the secp256k1 curve - cxNewSeckey, cxSeckeyIsValid,
-- cxPublicKey, cxPubkeyDecompress, cxSign, cxVerify, cxSignRecoverable,
-- cxRecover, cxEcdh and their six length accessors.
-- Phase 4 scope (ABI 4): the two BIP-32 curve steps (cxSeckeyTweakAdd,
-- cxPubkeyTweakAdd) and the BIP-39 wordlist (cxBip39Wordlist + its length).
-- That is ALL of phase 4 that belongs here: the mnemonic packing, the path
-- parse and the xprv/xpub framing are byte shuffling and live in
-- src/coinxt.livecodescript, by the same C-vs-script rule as the encodings.
-- ABI 5 (2026-08-16): cnx_memzero, INTERNAL secret hygiene only - sWipeFree
-- below wipes every out-buffer before freeing it. No new public handler; see
-- "SECRET HYGIENE, HONESTLY" further down.
--
-- ==========================================================================
-- PHASE 2 AND 4 STATUS: CLOSED ON-ENGINE 2026-08-10 (see below for what the
-- native side already proved, and the last paragraph for what the engine run
-- added). tools/coin-kat.py drives
-- every phase-2 cnx_ entry point headless through ctypes and pins it to public
-- vectors: CoinXT reproduces four published RFC 6979 secp256k1 signatures byte
-- for byte, a CoinXT signature VERIFIES in the independent Python `ecdsa`
-- library, a signature that library made verifies in CoinXT, and cxRecover's
-- native side returns the signing key. That is the IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN phase-2
-- bar, met at the C level.
--
-- The phase-4 additions stand in exactly the same place: tools/coin-kat.py
-- walks BIP-32 test vector 1 through cnx_seckey_tweak_add and
-- cnx_pubkey_tweak_add and requires the published private key at every level
-- (with the public path agreeing with the private one), and it requires the
-- wordlist to hash to the SHA-256 BIP-39 publishes. Native side proven; the
-- wrappers below are not.
--
-- The engine pass over the eighteen PUBLIC handlers happened on 2026-08-10:
-- tests/coin-selftest.livecodescript ran folded into the suite harness
-- (tests/suite-selftest.livecodescript at the repository root), every handler
-- in this file was called by its real name, and the same-day re-run came back
-- 207/207 green (the one defect the first run found was a script-layer path
-- parse, not a handler here - see CLAUDE.md, "the trailing-separator
-- fail-open"). The two shapes this block said to watch first were both
-- answered on the side the code assumed: the CInt FLAG marshals (cxPublicKey's
-- compressed and uncompressed calls returned 33 and 65 bytes, distinct), and
-- Boolean returns work (cxVerify answered true for a good signature and false
-- for a tampered one, and cxSeckeyIsValid answered both ways too).
--
-- PHASE 1 STATUS: ENGINE PASS RECORDED 2026-08-08. This module was compiled, loaded
-- and exercised on a real OXT engine by tests/suite-selftest.livecodescript
-- (root of the suite), which ran green. Per IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md phase 1 is
-- done when cxKeccak256 and friends return the PINNED vectors from a real
-- engine, so THAT BAR IS MET AND PHASE 1 IS CLOSED. All five of the questions
-- this block used to pose were answered, and every one of them landed on the
-- side the code already assumed - no fallback below had to be taken:
--   1. COMPILES, LOADS, BINDS RESOLVE. Confirmed: the digests came back, which
--      they could not have done through a silent bind failure at load.
--   2. THE ABI GUARD HOLDS against the shipped binary (ABI 2). Confirmed
--      transitively, which is airtight here: every public cx* handler opens
--      with sPrepare(), and sPrepare() is the ENTIRE body of cxCheckABI(). Four
--      handlers returned without throwing, so _cnx_abi_version() marshalled and
--      matched. (cxCheckABI() was finally called BY NAME on 2026-08-10, by the
--      folded harness, and passed against the shipped binary.)
--   3. UIntSize AS A FOREIGN RETURN TYPE: WORKS. This was the genuinely novel
--      one - UIntSize was proven in this family only as a PARAMETER. Every hash
--      wrapper below sizes its output buffer from a _cnx_*_len() declared
--      `returns UIntSize`, so a mismarshalled length would have allocated the
--      wrong buffer and broken the digest. The digests are byte-exact,
--      therefore the length is exact. The CUInt fallback is NOT needed; leave
--      these declarations alone.
--   4. MCDataGetBytePtr ON AN EMPTY Data: WORKS. cxKeccak256("") returned
--      c5d2...a470 rather than throwing, so the engine marshals an empty Data
--      through the plain `Pointer` slot. The `optional Pointer` fallback is NOT
--      needed. (This is now OUR engine result, not an inherited SodiumXT one.)
--   5. THE VECTORS, byte for byte from script: keccak256 of "" and of "abc",
--      sha256("abc") against FIPS 180-4, ripemd160("abc"), and cxSha3_256
--      proven DISTINCT from cxKeccak256 (the aliasing trap this surface exists
--      to avoid). All exact.
--
-- STILL VERIFIED STATICALLY: 12 of the 16 public handlers were not called by
-- name in that run - cxCheckABI (see 2), the seven *Len accessors, cxSha512,
-- cxHmacSha256, cxHmacSha512, cxPbkdf2HmacSha512. They are built from the same
-- proven parts (sPrepare + sAlloc + a UIntSize length + sFinish), and
-- tools/coin-kat.py drives all of them headless through ctypes against public
-- vectors, but neither of those is an engine result for the handler itself.
-- Calling them from script is a small, worthwhile follow-up.
--
-- The conventions below are the family FFI law (CLAUDE.md, "FFI / C-ABI
-- conventions"), each one paid for once already:
--   * an LCB library is a bracketed block: it MUST end with `end library`;
--   * an LCB Data does NOT auto-bridge to a void* (it marshals as an opaque
--     MCDataRef). An IN buffer passes MCDataGetBytePtr + MCDataGetLength; an
--     OUT buffer is a raw block from the engine <builtin> MCMemoryAllocate,
--     passed as a real Pointer, copied back with MCDataCreateWithBytes;
--   * our lengths are C size_t, so they marshal as UIntSize, NOT CUInt. A
--     4-byte int into an 8-byte size slot on a 64-bit build corrupts the heap;
--   * <builtin> handlers resolve BY NAME, so they carry no leading underscore
--     (renaming one breaks the bind); our own foreign decls use _cnx_*;
--   * no foreign handler here RETURNS a bridged C string. The engine adopts and
--     later free()s such a pointer, so a static or library-owned return is
--     free()-on-static: heap corruption on the first call, and doubly dangerous
--     with key material. The shim returns only int status / size_t lengths;
--   * `unsafe ... end unsafe` brackets EVERY foreign call, and every `variable`
--     is declared at the TOP of its handler (a nested declaration has broken
--     whole-script compilation before);
--   * `use com.livecode.foreign` is required wherever a foreign type is named.
--
-- One deliberate difference from sodium.lcb, the model this file mirrors. The
-- SodiumXT shim returns bytes-written or -needed, so its LCB layer guesses a
-- capacity and retries. The CoinXT shim does not: every output here is a FIXED
-- size the shim itself reports (cnx_*_len), or, for PBKDF2, exactly the length
-- the caller asked for. So we ask the shim for the size, allocate exactly that,
-- and copy exactly that back. There is no -needed retry path to get wrong, and
-- no size is ever hardcoded in this file (libraries are allowed to change their
-- digest sizes; a hardcoded 32 is a buffer overflow waiting to happen).
--
-- SECRET HYGIENE, HONESTLY. cxPbkdf2HmacSha512 derives a BIP-39 seed, and
-- cxHmacSha512 (the BIP-32 derivation core: its output splits into a child
-- key tweak and a chaincode), cxSeckeyTweakAdd (a child PRIVATE key) and
-- cxEcdh (a shared secret point) likewise write key material into the raw
-- block this file allocates. The shim memzeros its own scratch, and since
-- ABI 5 this layer wipes that block too: sFinish routes every out-buffer
-- through sWipeFree, which calls the shim's cnx_memzero(ptr, len) - the
-- export an earlier revision of this note recorded as future work; it was a
-- shim change and therefore an ABI bump (4 -> 5), made together, not smuggled
-- in - before MCMemoryDeallocate, on the success path and on both error
-- paths. The wipe is UNCONDITIONAL rather than per handler, because deciding
-- "secret enough to wipe" at each call site is a judgment that fails open
-- when it is wrong and saves one cheap call when it is right. The honest
-- residue is unchanged: the returned Data is ordinary engine memory - OXT
-- script variables are not locked (mlock) memory, can be paged to disk, and
-- cannot be wiped from here - so the caller still clears its own variable the
-- moment it is done. CoinXT on a general-purpose desktop is not
-- hardware-wallet isolation; the trust boundary is the machine (CLAUDE.md,
-- "Secret hygiene").
-- ==========================================================================

use com.livecode.foreign
use com.livecode.string
use com.livecode.arithmetic

metadata version is "0.1.0"
metadata author  is "Seth Morrow Software"
metadata title   is "CoinXT"
metadata summary is "Bitcoin and Ethereum cryptography for xTalk via trezor-crypto (hashes, HMAC, PBKDF2; curve, HD wallet and address layers to follow)."

-- Must equal CNX_ABI_VERSION in native/coinxt.c; cxCheckABI() throws on skew.
-- Bump BOTH in the same change, always (the family rule): a stale binary must
-- be refused with a legible error, not discovered at the first missing bind.
-- Declared before first use - OXT resolves a constant by lexical position, and
-- a forward reference silently evaluates to nothing.
constant kABIVersion is 5

-- The largest PBKDF2 iteration count this binding will marshal. The shim takes
-- a uint32_t, so 2^32-1 would technically fit, but 2^31-1 is the largest value
-- we can bounds-check unambiguously on the script side (an LCB Integer is
-- signed) and it is ~1e6 times any real-world count (BIP-39 uses 2048). The
-- check exists because a NEGATIVE or oversized Integer marshaled into an
-- unsigned foreign slot becomes a huge positive value, and the sign is only
-- still visible HERE.
constant kMaxIterations is 2147483647

-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Engine memory + Data bridges. These resolve by NAME against engine symbols,
-- so - unlike every other private handler in this file - they carry NO leading
-- underscore. Renaming any of them breaks the binding.
--   * MCMemoryAllocate / MCMemoryDeallocate: the raw writable block an OUT
--     buffer needs. The size is C size_t, hence UIntSize (see the header).
--   * MCDataGetBytePtr: the read-only pointer to a Data's own bytes, for the
--     IN buffers the shim reads. Never written through (it is a const byte_t*).
--   * MCDataGetLength / MCDataCreateWithBytes: a Data's byte count is the
--     engine's uindex_t == uint32, hence CUInt on both (NOT UIntSize - this is
--     the engine's own width here, not a C size_t).
-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
private foreign handler MCMemoryAllocate(in pSize as UIntSize, out rBlock as Pointer) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCMemoryDeallocate(in pBlock as Pointer) returns nothing binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCDataGetBytePtr(in pData as Data) returns Pointer binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCDataGetLength(in pData as Data) returns CUInt binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCDataCreateWithBytes(in pBytes as Pointer, in pCount as CUInt, out rData as Data) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"

-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The CoinXT C ABI (native/coinxt.c). Private _cnx_* names; the bind strings
-- reference the stable exported symbols by name, and those symbols are NEVER
-- renamed once shipped. Every entry returns an int status (0 ok, negative
-- error); every buffer crosses as Pointer + a size_t length (UIntSize).
-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
private foreign handler _cnx_abi_version() returns CInt binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_abi_version"

-- The ABI 5 secret-hygiene export: wipe pLen bytes at pPtr with a wipe the
-- compiler cannot elide (vendor/memzero.c: SecureZeroMemory / memset_s /
-- explicit_bzero / a volatile byte loop, per platform). INTERNAL: sWipeFree is
-- its only caller and there is deliberately no public cx* wrapper - a script
-- Data cannot be wiped in place, so exposing this to script would only invite
-- the false belief that it can (see "SECRET HYGIENE, HONESTLY" above).
private foreign handler _cnx_memzero(in pPtr as Pointer, in pLen as UIntSize) returns CInt binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_memzero"

-- The one-shot digests. `in` may be a NULL pointer when its length is 0 (the
-- shim substitutes a valid source), so an empty Data is always safe to hash.
-- Keccak-256 is NOT SHA3-256: Ethereum kept the original 0x01 padding, FIPS-202
-- uses 0x06. Two different functions, never aliased - the classic footgun.
private foreign handler _cnx_keccak256(in pIn as Pointer, in pInLen as UIntSize, in pOut as Pointer) returns CInt binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_keccak256"
private foreign handler _cnx_sha3_256(in pIn as Pointer, in pInLen as UIntSize, in pOut as Pointer) returns CInt binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_sha3_256"
private foreign handler _cnx_sha256(in pIn as Pointer, in pInLen as UIntSize, in pOut as Pointer) returns CInt binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_sha256"
private foreign handler _cnx_sha512(in pIn as Pointer, in pInLen as UIntSize, in pOut as Pointer) returns CInt binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_sha512"
private foreign handler _cnx_ripemd160(in pIn as Pointer, in pInLen as UIntSize, in pOut as Pointer) returns CInt binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_ripemd160"

-- Keyed MACs (RFC 2104). Key and message may each be empty; an over-long key is
-- hashed down by the primitive, so there is no key-length rule to enforce here.
private foreign handler _cnx_hmac_sha256(in pKey as Pointer, in pKeyLen as UIntSize, in pMsg as Pointer, in pMsgLen as UIntSize, in pOut as Pointer) returns CInt binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_hmac_sha256"
private foreign handler _cnx_hmac_sha512(in pKey as Pointer, in pKeyLen as UIntSize, in pMsg as Pointer, in pMsgLen as UIntSize, in pOut as Pointer) returns CInt binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_hmac_sha512"

-- The BIP-39 mnemonic-to-seed KDF. The iteration count is a C uint32_t (CUInt),
-- NOT a size_t: it is the one length-ish argument here that is not UIntSize, and
-- getting that backwards is exactly the heap bug the family rule warns about.
private foreign handler _cnx_pbkdf2_hmac_sha512(in pPw as Pointer, in pPwLen as UIntSize, in pSalt as Pointer, in pSaltLen as UIntSize, in pIterations as CUInt, in pOut as Pointer, in pOutLen as UIntSize) returns CInt binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_pbkdf2_hmac_sha512"

-- Every length is a function of the shim's, never a constant of ours. These
-- return C size_t, hence UIntSize (see point 3 in the header note).
private foreign handler _cnx_keccak256_len() returns UIntSize binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_keccak256_len"
private foreign handler _cnx_sha3_256_len() returns UIntSize binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_sha3_256_len"
private foreign handler _cnx_sha256_len() returns UIntSize binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_sha256_len"
private foreign handler _cnx_sha512_len() returns UIntSize binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_sha512_len"
private foreign handler _cnx_ripemd160_len() returns UIntSize binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_ripemd160_len"
private foreign handler _cnx_hmac_sha256_len() returns UIntSize binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_hmac_sha256_len"
private foreign handler _cnx_hmac_sha512_len() returns UIntSize binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_hmac_sha512_len"

-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The secp256k1 curve surface (phase 2).
--
-- Every public key argument crosses as a POINTER PLUS ITS LENGTH, and the shim
-- refuses any pair where the two disagree. That is not defensive padding: the
-- upstream parser dispatches on the leading byte and reads 65 bytes whenever it
-- sees 0x04, without ever being told the buffer size, so a 33-byte key whose
-- first byte got flipped to 0x04 would be read 32 bytes past its end. The
-- length is the only thing that can stop it, and it has to be carried here.
--
-- pCompressed is a C int (0/1), not a CBool: the family rule is that booleans
-- cross as int, and this layer converts an LCB Boolean to 1 or 0 explicitly
-- rather than trusting a bridged bool width.
-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
private foreign handler _cnx_seckey_verify(in pSk as Pointer, in pSkLen as UIntSize) returns CInt binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_seckey_verify"
private foreign handler _cnx_pubkey_from_seckey(in pSk as Pointer, in pSkLen as UIntSize, in pCompressed as CInt, in pOut as Pointer, in pOutLen as UIntSize) returns CInt binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_pubkey_from_seckey"
private foreign handler _cnx_pubkey_decompress(in pPub as Pointer, in pPubLen as UIntSize, in pOut as Pointer, in pOutLen as UIntSize) returns CInt binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_pubkey_decompress"
private foreign handler _cnx_ecdsa_sign(in pSk as Pointer, in pSkLen as UIntSize, in pDigest as Pointer, in pDigestLen as UIntSize, in pOut as Pointer, in pOutLen as UIntSize) returns CInt binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_ecdsa_sign"
private foreign handler _cnx_ecdsa_verify(in pPub as Pointer, in pPubLen as UIntSize, in pDigest as Pointer, in pDigestLen as UIntSize, in pSig as Pointer, in pSigLen as UIntSize) returns CInt binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_ecdsa_verify"
private foreign handler _cnx_ecdsa_sign_recoverable(in pSk as Pointer, in pSkLen as UIntSize, in pDigest as Pointer, in pDigestLen as UIntSize, in pOut as Pointer, in pOutLen as UIntSize) returns CInt binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_ecdsa_sign_recoverable"
private foreign handler _cnx_ecdsa_recover(in pSig as Pointer, in pSigLen as UIntSize, in pDigest as Pointer, in pDigestLen as UIntSize, in pOut as Pointer, in pOutLen as UIntSize) returns CInt binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_ecdsa_recover"
private foreign handler _cnx_ecdh(in pSk as Pointer, in pSkLen as UIntSize, in pPub as Pointer, in pPubLen as UIntSize, in pOut as Pointer, in pOutLen as UIntSize) returns CInt binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_ecdh"

private foreign handler _cnx_seckey_len() returns UIntSize binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_seckey_len"
private foreign handler _cnx_pubkey_len_compressed() returns UIntSize binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_pubkey_len_compressed"
private foreign handler _cnx_pubkey_len_uncompressed() returns UIntSize binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_pubkey_len_uncompressed"
private foreign handler _cnx_ecdsa_sig_len() returns UIntSize binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_ecdsa_sig_len"
private foreign handler _cnx_recoverable_sig_len() returns UIntSize binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_recoverable_sig_len"
private foreign handler _cnx_ecdh_len() returns UIntSize binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_ecdh_len"

-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Phase 4: the two curve steps BIP-32 needs, and the BIP-39 wordlist.
--
-- The shim exports only the parts of BIP-32 that ARE curve arithmetic -
-- ki = (IL + kpar) mod n and Ki = point(IL) + Kpar. Everything else about HD
-- derivation (the HMAC-SHA512, the serialization, the path parse) is byte
-- shuffling and lives in src/coinxt.livecodescript, per the C-vs-script split
-- in CLAUDE.md. See the long note above these two in native/coinxt.c for why
-- upstream's whole bip32.c was not vendored instead.
--
-- The wordlist crosses as ONE 16384-byte Data: 2048 slots of 8 bytes, each a
-- word left-aligned and space padded, in list order. It is normative data, so
-- it is vendored verbatim rather than transcribed into a script constant; the
-- fixed width is what lets the script index it with a chunk expression and
-- binary-search it instead of splitting 15 KB on every lookup.
-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
private foreign handler _cnx_seckey_tweak_add(in pSk as Pointer, in pSkLen as UIntSize, in pTweak as Pointer, in pTweakLen as UIntSize, in pOut as Pointer, in pOutLen as UIntSize) returns CInt binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_seckey_tweak_add"
private foreign handler _cnx_pubkey_tweak_add(in pPub as Pointer, in pPubLen as UIntSize, in pTweak as Pointer, in pTweakLen as UIntSize, in pOut as Pointer, in pOutLen as UIntSize) returns CInt binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_pubkey_tweak_add"
private foreign handler _cnx_bip39_wordlist(in pOut as Pointer, in pOutLen as UIntSize) returns CInt binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_bip39_wordlist"
private foreign handler _cnx_bip39_wordlist_len() returns UIntSize binds to "c:coinxt>cnx_bip39_wordlist_len"

-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Private helpers. Each wraps its foreign call(s) in `unsafe`, so the public
-- handlers stay readable and the foreign-call discipline lives in one place.
-- All declarations sit at the top of their handler.
-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Refuse a mismatched native library BEFORE any call that could corrupt memory.
-- Called by every public handler (sodium.lcb does the same), deliberately NOT
-- memoized in a module variable: the default value of an unassigned module
-- variable is not something we can verify headlessly, and one extra call to a
-- function that returns a compile-time constant is far cheaper than being wrong
-- about which binary is loaded in a library that handles money.
private handler sPrepare() returns nothing
   variable tABI as Integer
   unsafe
      put _cnx_abi_version() into tABI
   end unsafe
   if tABI is not kABIVersion then
      throw "CoinXT: ABI mismatch - the native coinxt library does not match this extension. Reinstall CoinXT (the packaged extension and its bundled library must ship together)."
   end if
end handler

private handler sAlloc(in pCap as Integer) returns Pointer
   variable tPtr as Pointer
   variable tOk as CBool
   unsafe
      put MCMemoryAllocate(pCap, tPtr) into tOk
   end unsafe
   if not tOk then
      throw "CoinXT: out of memory allocating an output buffer."
   end if
   return tPtr
end handler

-- Wipe, then free, one raw out-buffer. EVERY block this file allocates leaves
-- through here (sFinish routes all three of its paths this way), so a freed
-- block never re-enters the allocator still holding a seed, a child private
-- key, a chaincode half or an ECDH point. The wipe is the shim's cnx_memzero,
-- which the compiler cannot elide the way it could a store the block never
-- reads again; see "SECRET HYGIENE, HONESTLY" in the header. The status check
-- is a belt-and-braces impossibility guard, not a real branch: pPtr comes from
-- sAlloc (never null) and sPrepare() has already proven the loaded library
-- speaks this ABI, so cnx_memzero cannot refuse - but a status this file did
-- not check would be the one silent path in it, so it is checked. The free has
-- already happened by then, deliberately: wipe, free, THEN judge, so even the
-- impossible path leaks nothing.
private handler sWipeFree(in pPtr as Pointer, in pLen as Integer) returns nothing
   variable tStatus as Integer
   unsafe
      put _cnx_memzero(pPtr, pLen) into tStatus
      MCMemoryDeallocate(pPtr)
   end unsafe
   if tStatus is not 0 then
      throw "CoinXT: the native wipe refused a buffer this extension allocated - the loaded coinxt library does not match this binding. Reinstall CoinXT."
   end if
end handler

-- The read-only pointer to a Data's own bytes. On an EMPTY Data this may be a
-- null pointer; that is safe here because every shim entry accepts NULL when
-- the matching length is 0 (it substitutes a valid one-byte source rather than
-- letting a hash internal dereference NULL).
private handler sDataPtr(in pData as Data) returns Pointer
   variable tPtr as Pointer
   unsafe
      put MCDataGetBytePtr(pData) into tPtr
   end unsafe
   return tPtr
end handler

private handler sDataLen(in pData as Data) returns Integer
   variable tLen as Integer
   unsafe
      put MCDataGetLength(pData) into tLen
   end unsafe
   return tLen
end handler

-- Map a native status to a human sentence. The set is closed (the shim defines
-- exactly these), and the code number is deliberately NOT interpolated into the
-- message: LCB number-to-String coercion inside a concatenation is not something
-- this layer can verify headlessly, and an unrecognised status can only mean a
-- library newer than this binding - which cxCheckABI() / sPrepare() would
-- already have refused.
private handler sStatusText(in pStatus as Integer) returns String
   if pStatus is -1 then
      return "a required buffer was missing (null)."
   end if
   if pStatus is -2 then
      return "a buffer had the wrong length."
   end if
   if pStatus is -3 then
      return "a length or count is out of the range the native primitive can represent."
   end if
   if pStatus is -4 then
      return "the key is not a valid secp256k1 key."
   end if
   if pStatus is -5 then
      return "the signature is malformed or does not verify."
   end if
   if pStatus is -6 then
      return "the operating system entropy source is unavailable, so the side-channel blinding the curve code requires could not be drawn."
   end if
   if pStatus is -7 then
      return "the digest is all zero, which is refused because such a signature can be forged for any key."
   end if
   if pStatus is -8 then
      return "the native curve library failed unexpectedly."
   end if
   return "an unexpected native status (the loaded library does not match this binding)."
end handler

-- The common tail of a buffer-filling call: on a non-zero status wipe and free
-- the block, then throw a prefixed, handler-named error a caller can
-- try/catch; on success copy exactly the bytes the shim promised to write,
-- then wipe and free. pLen always comes from the shim (a cnx_*_len, or the
-- caller's requested PBKDF2 output), never from a constant in this file. The
-- copy is made inline rather than through a helper that throws, so the block
-- is wiped and freed BEFORE any throw this handler can raise - the old shape
-- (a throwing sToData between allocate and free) would have leaked the block
-- unwiped on the copy-failure path, which for a seed is the exact hole the
-- wipe exists to close.
private handler sFinish(in pName as String, in pPtr as Pointer, in pStatus as Integer, in pLen as Integer) returns Data
   variable tResult as Data
   variable tOk as CBool
   if pStatus is not 0 then
      sWipeFree(pPtr, pLen)
      throw "CoinXT:" && pName & ":" && sStatusText(pStatus)
   end if
   unsafe
      put MCDataCreateWithBytes(pPtr, pLen, tResult) into tOk
   end unsafe
   sWipeFree(pPtr, pLen)
   if not tOk then
      throw "CoinXT: could not build the result data."
   end if
   return tResult
end handler

-- ==========================================================================
-- The ABI guard.
-- ==========================================================================

-- Call this once at startup (or let any cx* call do it for you) to fail loudly
-- and legibly on a stale or mismatched native library.
public handler cxCheckABI() returns nothing
   sPrepare()
end handler

-- ==========================================================================
-- Digests. Bytes in, bytes out; an empty input is valid everywhere.
-- ==========================================================================

-- Keccak-256, Ethereum's hash (original 0x01 padding). This is what an Ethereum
-- address, an EIP-55 checksum, an ABI selector and a transaction hash are built
-- from. It is NOT SHA3-256; do not substitute one for the other.
public handler cxKeccak256(in pData as Data) returns Data
   variable tLen as Integer
   variable tOut as Pointer
   variable tInPtr as Pointer
   variable tInLen as Integer
   variable tStatus as Integer
   sPrepare()
   put sDataLen(pData) into tInLen
   put sDataPtr(pData) into tInPtr
   unsafe
      put _cnx_keccak256_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   put sAlloc(tLen) into tOut
   unsafe
      put _cnx_keccak256(tInPtr, tInLen, tOut) into tStatus
   end unsafe
   return sFinish("cxKeccak256", tOut, tStatus, tLen)
end handler

-- SHA3-256, the NIST FIPS-202 function (0x06 padding). Present so an app that
-- genuinely wants standard SHA-3 does not reach for cxKeccak256 by mistake.
public handler cxSha3_256(in pData as Data) returns Data
   variable tLen as Integer
   variable tOut as Pointer
   variable tInPtr as Pointer
   variable tInLen as Integer
   variable tStatus as Integer
   sPrepare()
   put sDataLen(pData) into tInLen
   put sDataPtr(pData) into tInPtr
   unsafe
      put _cnx_sha3_256_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   put sAlloc(tLen) into tOut
   unsafe
      put _cnx_sha3_256(tInPtr, tInLen, tOut) into tStatus
   end unsafe
   return sFinish("cxSha3_256", tOut, tStatus, tLen)
end handler

-- SHA-256. Bitcoin's workhorse: the Base58Check checksum is the first 4 bytes
-- of SHA256(SHA256(x)), and hash160 is RIPEMD160(SHA256(pubkey)).
public handler cxSha256(in pData as Data) returns Data
   variable tLen as Integer
   variable tOut as Pointer
   variable tInPtr as Pointer
   variable tInLen as Integer
   variable tStatus as Integer
   sPrepare()
   put sDataLen(pData) into tInLen
   put sDataPtr(pData) into tInPtr
   unsafe
      put _cnx_sha256_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   put sAlloc(tLen) into tOut
   unsafe
      put _cnx_sha256(tInPtr, tInLen, tOut) into tStatus
   end unsafe
   return sFinish("cxSha256", tOut, tStatus, tLen)
end handler

-- SHA-512. What BIP-32 and BIP-39 derive with (via HMAC / PBKDF2 below).
public handler cxSha512(in pData as Data) returns Data
   variable tLen as Integer
   variable tOut as Pointer
   variable tInPtr as Pointer
   variable tInLen as Integer
   variable tStatus as Integer
   sPrepare()
   put sDataLen(pData) into tInLen
   put sDataPtr(pData) into tInPtr
   unsafe
      put _cnx_sha512_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   put sAlloc(tLen) into tOut
   unsafe
      put _cnx_sha512(tInPtr, tInLen, tOut) into tStatus
   end unsafe
   return sFinish("cxSha512", tOut, tStatus, tLen)
end handler

-- RIPEMD-160. The second half of Bitcoin's hash160 (P2PKH / P2WPKH addresses).
public handler cxRipemd160(in pData as Data) returns Data
   variable tLen as Integer
   variable tOut as Pointer
   variable tInPtr as Pointer
   variable tInLen as Integer
   variable tStatus as Integer
   sPrepare()
   put sDataLen(pData) into tInLen
   put sDataPtr(pData) into tInPtr
   unsafe
      put _cnx_ripemd160_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   put sAlloc(tLen) into tOut
   unsafe
      put _cnx_ripemd160(tInPtr, tInLen, tOut) into tStatus
   end unsafe
   return sFinish("cxRipemd160", tOut, tStatus, tLen)
end handler

-- ==========================================================================
-- Keyed MACs. Both arguments may be empty; both are read, never written.
-- ==========================================================================

-- HMAC-SHA256 (RFC 2104). Used by the Tor control-port SAFECOOKIE exchange and
-- as a generic domain-separated MAC.
public handler cxHmacSha256(in pKey as Data, in pMessage as Data) returns Data
   variable tLen as Integer
   variable tOut as Pointer
   variable tKeyPtr as Pointer
   variable tKeyLen as Integer
   variable tMsgPtr as Pointer
   variable tMsgLen as Integer
   variable tStatus as Integer
   sPrepare()
   put sDataLen(pKey) into tKeyLen
   put sDataPtr(pKey) into tKeyPtr
   put sDataLen(pMessage) into tMsgLen
   put sDataPtr(pMessage) into tMsgPtr
   unsafe
      put _cnx_hmac_sha256_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   put sAlloc(tLen) into tOut
   unsafe
      put _cnx_hmac_sha256(tKeyPtr, tKeyLen, tMsgPtr, tMsgLen, tOut) into tStatus
   end unsafe
   return sFinish("cxHmacSha256", tOut, tStatus, tLen)
end handler

-- HMAC-SHA512 (RFC 2104). This is the BIP-32 derivation core: I = HMAC-SHA512
-- (chaincode, data), split 32/32 into the child key tweak and the new chaincode.
-- The key here can be a chaincode - secret material - so the caller should clear
-- its own variable as soon as it is done (see the header note on hygiene).
public handler cxHmacSha512(in pKey as Data, in pMessage as Data) returns Data
   variable tLen as Integer
   variable tOut as Pointer
   variable tKeyPtr as Pointer
   variable tKeyLen as Integer
   variable tMsgPtr as Pointer
   variable tMsgLen as Integer
   variable tStatus as Integer
   sPrepare()
   put sDataLen(pKey) into tKeyLen
   put sDataPtr(pKey) into tKeyPtr
   put sDataLen(pMessage) into tMsgLen
   put sDataPtr(pMessage) into tMsgPtr
   unsafe
      put _cnx_hmac_sha512_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   put sAlloc(tLen) into tOut
   unsafe
      put _cnx_hmac_sha512(tKeyPtr, tKeyLen, tMsgPtr, tMsgLen, tOut) into tStatus
   end unsafe
   return sFinish("cxHmacSha512", tOut, tStatus, tLen)
end handler

-- ==========================================================================
-- PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA512 (the BIP-39 mnemonic-to-seed KDF).
-- ==========================================================================

-- BIP-39 calls this as: password = the NFKD mnemonic sentence, salt =
-- "mnemonic" & the passphrase, iterations = 2048, output = 64 bytes. Text is
-- textEncode()d to UTF-8 in the livecodescript layer, because textEncode is not
-- available to an LCB module; this layer only ever sees Data.
--
-- The three checks below are NOT redundant with the shim's. The shim refuses a
-- zero iteration count and a zero output length (upstream would be quietly
-- WRONG rather than loud in both cases: a zero count yields the one-iteration
-- key, and a zero length "succeeds" having written nothing). But a NEGATIVE
-- Integer would never reach those checks intact - it becomes a huge positive
-- value the moment it marshals into an unsigned foreign slot - so its sign has
-- to be caught here, on the script side, while it still exists.
public handler cxPbkdf2HmacSha512(in pPassword as Data, in pSalt as Data, in pIterations as Integer, in pOutLen as Integer) returns Data
   variable tOut as Pointer
   variable tPwPtr as Pointer
   variable tPwLen as Integer
   variable tSaltPtr as Pointer
   variable tSaltLen as Integer
   variable tStatus as Integer
   sPrepare()
   if pOutLen < 1 then
      throw "CoinXT: cxPbkdf2HmacSha512: the output length must be at least 1 byte (BIP-39 uses 64)."
   end if
   if pIterations < 1 then
      throw "CoinXT: cxPbkdf2HmacSha512: the iteration count must be at least 1 (BIP-39 uses 2048); a count of 0 would silently derive a one-iteration key."
   end if
   if pIterations > kMaxIterations then
      throw "CoinXT: cxPbkdf2HmacSha512: the iteration count is larger than this binding will marshal."
   end if
   put sDataLen(pPassword) into tPwLen
   put sDataPtr(pPassword) into tPwPtr
   put sDataLen(pSalt) into tSaltLen
   put sDataPtr(pSalt) into tSaltPtr
   put sAlloc(pOutLen) into tOut
   unsafe
      put _cnx_pbkdf2_hmac_sha512(tPwPtr, tPwLen, tSaltPtr, tSaltLen, pIterations, tOut, pOutLen) into tStatus
   end unsafe
   -- The result is a SEED. sFinish wipes the raw block (cnx_memzero) before
   -- freeing it, but the returned Data is not locked memory - see the header.
   -- Clear the caller's variable when done with it.
   return sFinish("cxPbkdf2HmacSha512", tOut, tStatus, pOutLen)
end handler

-- ==========================================================================
-- The secp256k1 curve (phase 2). Keys, ECDSA, recoverable ECDSA, recovery, ECDH.
--
-- THE CUSTODY BOUNDARY, stated once and meant everywhere below. CoinXT is a
-- calculator: it holds a private key for the microseconds of one call and keeps
-- nothing. Storage, backup, and confirm-before-sign are the app's. Clear your
-- own key variables (`put empty into tSeckey`) the moment you are done, and read
-- the header note on what that does and does not buy you.
--
-- SIGN ONLY WHAT YOU BUILT. cxSign takes a 32-byte DIGEST, never a message and
-- never a transaction. Constructing the right sighash (and showing the user what
-- it means) is the app's job, because a signer that will sign any 32 bytes handed
-- to it is a footgun aimed at whoever installed your app.
-- ==========================================================================

-- Validate 32 bytes of CALLER entropy as a secp256k1 private key and hand them
-- back. There is no transformation: a valid key just IS a 32-byte integer in
-- [1, n-1], and roughly one draw in 2^128 fails, so the honest API is "check it
-- and give it back" rather than something that looks like generation.
--
-- The entropy is yours to supply and must be cryptographically random: compose
-- SodiumXT's sxRandomBytes(32) or the OS. CoinXT deliberately has no key-making
-- RNG of its own, so nothing here can quietly hand you a weak key.
public handler cxNewSeckey(in pEntropy as Data) returns Data
   variable tPtr as Pointer
   variable tLen as Integer
   variable tStatus as Integer
   sPrepare()
   put sDataLen(pEntropy) into tLen
   put sDataPtr(pEntropy) into tPtr
   unsafe
      put _cnx_seckey_verify(tPtr, tLen) into tStatus
   end unsafe
   if tStatus is not 0 then
      throw "CoinXT: cxNewSeckey:" && sStatusText(tStatus)
   end if
   return pEntropy
end handler

-- The same question asked without exceptions, for an IMPORTED key (a WIF, a
-- pasted hex string) where "no" is an ordinary answer and not an error. Every
-- way of failing to be a key - empty, wrong length, zero, >= the group order -
-- answers false; anything else still throws, because it would mean the loaded
-- library is not the one this binding was built against.
public handler cxSeckeyIsValid(in pSeckey as Data) returns Boolean
   variable tPtr as Pointer
   variable tLen as Integer
   variable tStatus as Integer
   sPrepare()
   put sDataLen(pSeckey) into tLen
   put sDataPtr(pSeckey) into tPtr
   unsafe
      put _cnx_seckey_verify(tPtr, tLen) into tStatus
   end unsafe
   if tStatus is 0 then
      return true
   end if
   if tStatus is -1 or tStatus is -2 or tStatus is -4 then
      return false
   end if
   throw "CoinXT: cxSeckeyIsValid:" && sStatusText(tStatus)
end handler

-- The public key for a private key. Compressed (33 bytes, 0x02/0x03 || X) is
-- what modern Bitcoin uses everywhere; uncompressed (65 bytes, 0x04 || X || Y)
-- is what an Ethereum address is built from (Keccak-256 of the 64 bytes AFTER
-- the 0x04, last 20 bytes). Both sizes come from the shim, never from here.
public handler cxPublicKey(in pSeckey as Data, in pCompressed as Boolean) returns Data
   variable tLen as Integer
   variable tLenCompressed as Integer
   variable tLenUncompressed as Integer
   variable tOut as Pointer
   variable tSkPtr as Pointer
   variable tSkLen as Integer
   variable tStatus as Integer
   variable tFlag as Integer
   sPrepare()
   put sDataLen(pSeckey) into tSkLen
   put sDataPtr(pSeckey) into tSkPtr
   unsafe
      put _cnx_pubkey_len_compressed() into tLenCompressed
      put _cnx_pubkey_len_uncompressed() into tLenUncompressed
   end unsafe
   -- Resolved OUTSIDE the unsafe block on purpose: the foreign calls stay
   -- straight-line, and the 0/1 the C int wants is produced explicitly here
   -- rather than by trusting a bridged boolean width.
   if pCompressed then
      put tLenCompressed into tLen
      put 1 into tFlag
   else
      put tLenUncompressed into tLen
      put 0 into tFlag
   end if
   put sAlloc(tLen) into tOut
   unsafe
      put _cnx_pubkey_from_seckey(tSkPtr, tSkLen, tFlag, tOut, tLen) into tStatus
   end unsafe
   return sFinish("cxPublicKey", tOut, tStatus, tLen)
end handler

-- Expand a compressed public key to its 65-byte form (solving the curve
-- equation for Y). Feeding it an already-uncompressed key is legal and returns
-- it unchanged, so a caller normalising mixed input does not have to branch.
public handler cxPubkeyDecompress(in pPubkey as Data) returns Data
   variable tLen as Integer
   variable tOut as Pointer
   variable tPubPtr as Pointer
   variable tPubLen as Integer
   variable tStatus as Integer
   sPrepare()
   put sDataLen(pPubkey) into tPubLen
   put sDataPtr(pPubkey) into tPubPtr
   unsafe
      put _cnx_pubkey_len_uncompressed() into tLen
   end unsafe
   put sAlloc(tLen) into tOut
   unsafe
      put _cnx_pubkey_decompress(tPubPtr, tPubLen, tOut, tLen) into tStatus
   end unsafe
   return sFinish("cxPubkeyDecompress", tOut, tStatus, tLen)
end handler

-- ECDSA over a 32-byte digest, RFC 6979 deterministic. The same key and digest
-- always produce the same 64 bytes (r || s), which is why the whole surface is
-- KAT-pinnable; the native code still draws OS entropy for side-channel
-- blinding, and that blinding provably cancels out of the result.
--
-- s is always the LOW of the two equivalent values, which Bitcoin requires for
-- relay (BIP-62) and Ethereum requires for consensus. That is upstream's doing,
-- not a normalisation added here.
public handler cxSign(in pSeckey as Data, in pDigest as Data) returns Data
   variable tLen as Integer
   variable tOut as Pointer
   variable tSkPtr as Pointer
   variable tSkLen as Integer
   variable tDigPtr as Pointer
   variable tDigLen as Integer
   variable tStatus as Integer
   sPrepare()
   put sDataLen(pSeckey) into tSkLen
   put sDataPtr(pSeckey) into tSkPtr
   put sDataLen(pDigest) into tDigLen
   put sDataPtr(pDigest) into tDigPtr
   unsafe
      put _cnx_ecdsa_sig_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   put sAlloc(tLen) into tOut
   unsafe
      put _cnx_ecdsa_sign(tSkPtr, tSkLen, tDigPtr, tDigLen, tOut, tLen) into tStatus
   end unsafe
   return sFinish("cxSign", tOut, tStatus, tLen)
end handler

-- Verify a 64-byte signature. This returns a BOOLEAN and throws for everything
-- else, and the split is deliberate: "this signature does not verify" is the
-- normal, expected answer that callers branch on, while a malformed public key
-- or a 63-byte signature is a BUG in the calling code. A verify that threw on an
-- invalid signature would be unusable; one that answered false to a malformed
-- key would hide the bug behind a security-shaped result.
public handler cxVerify(in pPubkey as Data, in pDigest as Data, in pSignature as Data) returns Boolean
   variable tPubPtr as Pointer
   variable tPubLen as Integer
   variable tDigPtr as Pointer
   variable tDigLen as Integer
   variable tSigPtr as Pointer
   variable tSigLen as Integer
   variable tStatus as Integer
   sPrepare()
   put sDataLen(pPubkey) into tPubLen
   put sDataPtr(pPubkey) into tPubPtr
   put sDataLen(pDigest) into tDigLen
   put sDataPtr(pDigest) into tDigPtr
   put sDataLen(pSignature) into tSigLen
   put sDataPtr(pSignature) into tSigPtr
   unsafe
      put _cnx_ecdsa_verify(tPubPtr, tPubLen, tDigPtr, tDigLen, tSigPtr, tSigLen) into tStatus
   end unsafe
   if tStatus is 0 then
      return true
   end if
   if tStatus is -5 then
      return false
   end if
   throw "CoinXT: cxVerify:" && sStatusText(tStatus)
end handler

-- The recoverable form: the same 64 bytes with a 1-byte recovery id appended
-- (65 total). Ethereum's `v` is built from that id, and it is what lets a
-- verifier recover the signer's key from the signature alone instead of being
-- told which key to check. The id is 0..3.
public handler cxSignRecoverable(in pSeckey as Data, in pDigest as Data) returns Data
   variable tLen as Integer
   variable tOut as Pointer
   variable tSkPtr as Pointer
   variable tSkLen as Integer
   variable tDigPtr as Pointer
   variable tDigLen as Integer
   variable tStatus as Integer
   sPrepare()
   put sDataLen(pSeckey) into tSkLen
   put sDataPtr(pSeckey) into tSkPtr
   put sDataLen(pDigest) into tDigLen
   put sDataPtr(pDigest) into tDigPtr
   unsafe
      put _cnx_recoverable_sig_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   put sAlloc(tLen) into tOut
   unsafe
      put _cnx_ecdsa_sign_recoverable(tSkPtr, tSkLen, tDigPtr, tDigLen, tOut, tLen) into tStatus
   end unsafe
   return sFinish("cxSignRecoverable", tOut, tStatus, tLen)
end handler

-- ecrecover: the 65-byte uncompressed public key that produced this recoverable
-- signature over this digest. Recovery ALWAYS produces some key for a
-- well-formed signature, so this answering successfully proves nothing on its
-- own; it is meaningful only when the recovered key is then compared against
-- the key you expected. That comparison is the caller's, and skipping it is the
-- classic ecrecover mistake.
public handler cxRecover(in pSignature as Data, in pDigest as Data) returns Data
   variable tLen as Integer
   variable tOut as Pointer
   variable tSigPtr as Pointer
   variable tSigLen as Integer
   variable tDigPtr as Pointer
   variable tDigLen as Integer
   variable tStatus as Integer
   sPrepare()
   put sDataLen(pSignature) into tSigLen
   put sDataPtr(pSignature) into tSigPtr
   put sDataLen(pDigest) into tDigLen
   put sDataPtr(pDigest) into tDigPtr
   unsafe
      put _cnx_pubkey_len_uncompressed() into tLen
   end unsafe
   put sAlloc(tLen) into tOut
   unsafe
      put _cnx_ecdsa_recover(tSigPtr, tSigLen, tDigPtr, tDigLen, tOut, tLen) into tStatus
   end unsafe
   return sFinish("cxRecover", tOut, tStatus, tLen)
end handler

-- ECDH: the shared point between your private key and their public key, as the
-- raw 65 bytes (0x04 || X || Y).
--
-- THIS IS NOT A SHARED KEY. Every protocol that uses ECDH runs a KDF over this
-- point first, and they disagree about which one and about whether it covers X
-- alone or the compressed form. Hashing it here would be CoinXT inventing a
-- convention and calling it interoperability, so the point is returned as
-- computed and the KDF is yours (cxSha256 of the 32 X bytes is a common one;
-- match whatever the protocol on the other end specifies).
public handler cxEcdh(in pSeckey as Data, in pPubkey as Data) returns Data
   variable tLen as Integer
   variable tOut as Pointer
   variable tSkPtr as Pointer
   variable tSkLen as Integer
   variable tPubPtr as Pointer
   variable tPubLen as Integer
   variable tStatus as Integer
   sPrepare()
   put sDataLen(pSeckey) into tSkLen
   put sDataPtr(pSeckey) into tSkPtr
   put sDataLen(pPubkey) into tPubLen
   put sDataPtr(pPubkey) into tPubPtr
   unsafe
      put _cnx_ecdh_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   put sAlloc(tLen) into tOut
   unsafe
      put _cnx_ecdh(tSkPtr, tSkLen, tPubPtr, tPubLen, tOut, tLen) into tStatus
   end unsafe
   return sFinish("cxEcdh", tOut, tStatus, tLen)
end handler

-- ==========================================================================
-- Phase 4: the curve steps of BIP-32, and the BIP-39 wordlist.
--
-- These three are LOW-LEVEL and most callers should not touch them: the HD
-- API a wallet wants (cxHdFromSeed, cxHdDerivePath, cxMnemonicToSeed and
-- friends) is in src/coinxt.livecodescript and is built out of exactly these.
-- They are public because that script layer is a separate file and can only
-- reach the shim through the module's public surface, and because a caller
-- implementing a non-BIP-32 scheme on the same curve has a legitimate use for
-- a tweak. Nothing here parses a path or knows what a chain code is.
-- ==========================================================================

-- ki = (pTweak + pSeckey) mod n, the private half of one BIP-32 derivation
-- step. pTweak is IL, the first 32 bytes of HMAC-SHA512(chain code, data).
--
-- Throws when the result is not a usable key - which BIP-32 defines as
-- IL >= n or ki = 0, and to which its answer is "use the next child index",
-- not "give up". A zero tweak throws for the same reason cxPubkeyTweakAdd
-- rejects one: see the note in native/coinxt.c. The probability of ever
-- meeting either is about 2^-128; handle it, do not design around it.
public handler cxSeckeyTweakAdd(in pSeckey as Data, in pTweak as Data) returns Data
   variable tLen as Integer
   variable tOut as Pointer
   variable tSkPtr as Pointer
   variable tSkLen as Integer
   variable tTweakPtr as Pointer
   variable tTweakLen as Integer
   variable tStatus as Integer
   sPrepare()
   put sDataLen(pSeckey) into tSkLen
   put sDataPtr(pSeckey) into tSkPtr
   put sDataLen(pTweak) into tTweakLen
   put sDataPtr(pTweak) into tTweakPtr
   unsafe
      put _cnx_seckey_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   put sAlloc(tLen) into tOut
   unsafe
      put _cnx_seckey_tweak_add(tSkPtr, tSkLen, tTweakPtr, tTweakLen, tOut, tLen) into tStatus
   end unsafe
   return sFinish("cxSeckeyTweakAdd", tOut, tStatus, tLen)
end handler

-- Ki = point(pTweak) + pPubkey, the public half of one BIP-32 derivation step,
-- returned COMPRESSED. This is what lets a watch-only wallet derive receive
-- addresses from an xpub with no private key present.
--
-- pPubkey must be the 33-byte compressed form. An uncompressed key throws
-- rather than being compressed on your behalf: BIP-32 hashes the COMPRESSED
-- parent into the HMAC, so a caller holding 65 bytes has already diverged from
-- the spec and needs to know it. Hardened children have no public derivation
-- at all (they hash the private key), which is a property of BIP-32, not a
-- limitation here - cxHdDerivePath in the script layer says so by name.
public handler cxPubkeyTweakAdd(in pPubkey as Data, in pTweak as Data) returns Data
   variable tLen as Integer
   variable tOut as Pointer
   variable tPubPtr as Pointer
   variable tPubLen as Integer
   variable tTweakPtr as Pointer
   variable tTweakLen as Integer
   variable tStatus as Integer
   sPrepare()
   put sDataLen(pPubkey) into tPubLen
   put sDataPtr(pPubkey) into tPubPtr
   put sDataLen(pTweak) into tTweakLen
   put sDataPtr(pTweak) into tTweakPtr
   unsafe
      put _cnx_pubkey_len_compressed() into tLen
   end unsafe
   put sAlloc(tLen) into tOut
   unsafe
      put _cnx_pubkey_tweak_add(tPubPtr, tPubLen, tTweakPtr, tTweakLen, tOut, tLen) into tStatus
   end unsafe
   return sFinish("cxPubkeyTweakAdd", tOut, tStatus, tLen)
end handler

-- The normative BIP-39 English wordlist as 2048 fixed-width 8-byte slots, each
-- a word left-aligned and space padded, in list order. 16384 bytes.
--
-- Word i (0-based) is bytes i*8+1 through i*8+8; trim the trailing spaces. The
-- list is sorted, so word -> index is a binary search. The script layer fetches
-- this ONCE and caches it; there is no reason for an app to call it directly
-- unless it is building its own mnemonic UI (an autocomplete, say).
public handler cxBip39Wordlist() returns Data
   variable tLen as Integer
   variable tOut as Pointer
   variable tStatus as Integer
   sPrepare()
   unsafe
      put _cnx_bip39_wordlist_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   put sAlloc(tLen) into tOut
   unsafe
      put _cnx_bip39_wordlist(tOut, tLen) into tStatus
   end unsafe
   return sFinish("cxBip39Wordlist", tOut, tStatus, tLen)
end handler

-- ==========================================================================
-- Length accessors. Every size an app needs to build a buffer comes FROM the
-- shim; nothing in the CoinXT stack hardcodes 32 / 64 / 20. The ones that earn
-- their keep in practice: cxSha256Len and cxRipemd160Len size a hash160 and a
-- Base58Check checksum, cxHmacSha512Len is the 64-byte BIP-32 I that splits
-- 32/32, cxSha512Len is the BIP-39 seed length, and cxKeccak256Len sizes an
-- Ethereum address (the last 20 bytes of a Keccak-256 digest). The remaining
-- two are exposed for symmetry, so no caller has to guess which digest has an
-- accessor and which does not.
-- ==========================================================================

public handler cxKeccak256Len() returns Integer
   variable tLen as Integer
   sPrepare()
   unsafe
      put _cnx_keccak256_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   return tLen
end handler

public handler cxSha3_256Len() returns Integer
   variable tLen as Integer
   sPrepare()
   unsafe
      put _cnx_sha3_256_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   return tLen
end handler

public handler cxSha256Len() returns Integer
   variable tLen as Integer
   sPrepare()
   unsafe
      put _cnx_sha256_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   return tLen
end handler

public handler cxSha512Len() returns Integer
   variable tLen as Integer
   sPrepare()
   unsafe
      put _cnx_sha512_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   return tLen
end handler

public handler cxRipemd160Len() returns Integer
   variable tLen as Integer
   sPrepare()
   unsafe
      put _cnx_ripemd160_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   return tLen
end handler

public handler cxHmacSha256Len() returns Integer
   variable tLen as Integer
   sPrepare()
   unsafe
      put _cnx_hmac_sha256_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   return tLen
end handler

public handler cxHmacSha512Len() returns Integer
   variable tLen as Integer
   sPrepare()
   unsafe
      put _cnx_hmac_sha512_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   return tLen
end handler

-- The curve sizes. cxSeckeyLen is how many entropy bytes to draw for
-- cxNewSeckey; the two pubkey lengths tell an encoder which form it holds
-- without inspecting the prefix byte; cxSignatureLen and
-- cxRecoverableSignatureLen differ by exactly the one recovery byte.
public handler cxSeckeyLen() returns Integer
   variable tLen as Integer
   sPrepare()
   unsafe
      put _cnx_seckey_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   return tLen
end handler

public handler cxPubkeyLenCompressed() returns Integer
   variable tLen as Integer
   sPrepare()
   unsafe
      put _cnx_pubkey_len_compressed() into tLen
   end unsafe
   return tLen
end handler

public handler cxPubkeyLenUncompressed() returns Integer
   variable tLen as Integer
   sPrepare()
   unsafe
      put _cnx_pubkey_len_uncompressed() into tLen
   end unsafe
   return tLen
end handler

public handler cxSignatureLen() returns Integer
   variable tLen as Integer
   sPrepare()
   unsafe
      put _cnx_ecdsa_sig_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   return tLen
end handler

public handler cxRecoverableSignatureLen() returns Integer
   variable tLen as Integer
   sPrepare()
   unsafe
      put _cnx_recoverable_sig_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   return tLen
end handler

-- The RAW ECDH point, not a key (see cxEcdh).
public handler cxEcdhLen() returns Integer
   variable tLen as Integer
   sPrepare()
   unsafe
      put _cnx_ecdh_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   return tLen
end handler

-- The size of the whole wordlist blob, 2048 * 8. Exposed for the same reason
-- as every other accessor here: the script layer derives the slot width from
-- this divided by 2048 rather than writing 8 down a second time.
public handler cxBip39WordlistLen() returns Integer
   variable tLen as Integer
   sPrepare()
   unsafe
      put _cnx_bip39_wordlist_len() into tLen
   end unsafe
   return tLen
end handler

end library
